Crisp ingredient index — data licence
=====================================

The files in this directory (entries.json, locales.json, names/*.json,
VERSION) form a database derived from Open Food Facts and other sources, and
are licensed separately from the Crisp source code (AGPL-3.0-only).

This database is made available under the Open Database License (ODbL) v1.0:
https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/

Any rights in individual contents of the database are licensed under the
Database Contents License (DbCL) v1.0:
https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/dbcl/1-0/

You may share, create, and adapt this database provided you attribute it,
license any adapted database you publicly use under the ODbL, and keep it
open, as the ODbL describes. A recipe that references an ingredient slug is a
Produced Work, not an adapted database.

Sources and attribution
-----------------------

- Open Food Facts ingredients taxonomy (https://world.openfoodfacts.org),
  © Open Food Facts contributors, available under the ODbL v1.0. Pinned
  snapshot: openfoodfacts-server commit
  7cf21f22bb1da219a6bbef3667eb0909ee48135e, taxonomies/food/ingredients.txt.

- USDA FoodData Central, SR Legacy (April 2018), U.S. Department of
  Agriculture, Agricultural Research Service — public domain (CC0 1.0).
  Used for densities.

- Traditional Chinese regional names (zh-Hant-TW, zh-Hant-HK) and the
  Simplified normalization of zh-Hans-CN are derived with OpenCC dictionaries
  (https://github.com/BYVoid/OpenCC, commit
  fae2a0a911c30764e669a7d8b0591cd8e852934a), © Carbo Kuo and contributors,
  Apache License 2.0.

- Regional English names and the entries and names in
  tools/ingredients/rules/overrides/ are contributed by the Crisp project and
  its users under the same ODbL v1.0 terms.

How the database is generated and maintained is described in
docs/ingredient-index.md.
